Fix lost interrupts on the sun4u Sabre PCI host bridge

A NetBSD/sparc64 guest on the `sun4u` machine wedges for good under
concurrent disk and network I/O. The console prints
`cmdide0:0:0: lost interrupt` and `hme0: device timeout`, QEMU spins at
100% CPU, and no device interrupt is delivered again.

`pci_sabre_set_irq()` tracks the pending interrupt in a single slot,
`irq_request`. Raising a PCI interrupt overwrites that slot even while a
request for a different INO, such as the onboard cmd646 IDE's OBIO
interrupt, is still unacknowledged. The guest's interrupt-clear write
for the first INO then no longer matches `irq_request` and is silently
dropped, so neither device's interrupt is ever cleared. The OBIO branch
already refuses to overwrite an outstanding request; this adds the same
guard to the PCI branch. The PCI level stays latched in `pci_irq_in`,
and `sabre_check_irqs()` submits it once the current request is
cleared.

Not upstream in QEMU. Taken from anyvm-org/netbsd-builder (MIT licence,
Copyright (c) 2025 Run Any VM anywhere - AnyVM.org):
https://github.com/anyvm-org/netbsd-builder/blob/HEAD/files/qemu-sabre-irq-clobber.patch

--- a/hw/pci-host/sabre.c
+++ b/hw/pci-host/sabre.c
@@ -308,7 +308,17 @@
     if (irq_num < 32) {
         if (level) {
             s->pci_irq_in |= 1ULL << irq_num;
-            if (s->pci_irq_map[irq_num >> 2] & PBM_PCI_IMR_ENABLED) {
+            /* Do not overwrite an outstanding request for a DIFFERENT ino:
+             * irq_request is a single slot, and clobbering it makes the
+             * guest's later interrupt-clear write (which must match
+             * irq_request) a silent no-op, permanently wedging both
+             * devices' interrupts (seen as NetBSD/OpenBSD "lost interrupt"
+             * storms when disk and NIC DMA complete concurrently). The
+             * level is latched in pci_irq_in; sabre_check_irqs() will
+             * submit it once the current request is acknowledged. */
+            if ((s->pci_irq_map[irq_num >> 2] & PBM_PCI_IMR_ENABLED) &&
+                (s->irq_request == NO_IRQ_REQUEST ||
+                 s->irq_request == irq_num)) {
                 sabre_set_request(s, irq_num);
             }
         } else {
